RG)/pre-Botzinger complex (preBotC) is the primary site of respiratory rhythm generation in the mammal&#x0201d;</article-title><source>J Appl Physiol</source><year>2006</year><volume>101</volume><fpage>689</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1152/japplphysiol.00476.2006</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Feldman JL, Janczewski WA (2006) The Last Word: Point:Counterpoint authors respond to commentaries on &#x0201c;the parafacial respiratory group (pFRG)/pre-Botzinger complex (preBotC) is the primary site of respiratory rhythm generation in the mammal&#x0201d;. J Appl Physiol 101:689 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16709655</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR6"><label>6.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Funke</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Dutschmann</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>M&#x000fc;ller</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Imaging of respiratory-related population activity with single-cell resolution</article-title><source>Am J Physiol</source><year>2007</year><volume>292</volume><fpage>C508</fpage><lpage>C516</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1152/ajpcell.00253.2006</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Funke F, Dutschmann M, M&#x000fc;ller M (2007) Imaging of respiratory-related population activity with single-cell resolution. Am J Physiol 292:C508&#x02013;C516 </citation></ref><ref id="CR7"><label>7.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gomeza</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>H&#x000fc;lsmann</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Ohno</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Eulenburg</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Szoke</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Richter</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Betz</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Inactivation of the glycine transporter 1 gene discloses vital role of glial glycine uptake in glycinergic inhibition</article-title><source>Neuron</source><year>2003</year><volume>40</volume><fpage>785</fpage><lpage>796</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0896-6273(03)00672-X</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Gomeza J, H&#x000fc;lsmann S, Ohno K, Eulenburg V, Szoke K, Richter D, Betz H (2003) Inactivation of the glycine transporter 1 gene discloses vital role of glial glycine uptake in glycinergic inhibition. Neuron 40:785&#x02013;796 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14622582</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR8"><label>8.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gomeza</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Ohno</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>H&#x000fc;lsmann</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Armsen</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Eulenburg</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Richter</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name><name><surname>Laube</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Betz</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Deletion of the mouse glycine transporter 2 results in a hyperekplexia phenotype and postnatal lethality</article-title><source>Neuron</source><year>2003</year><volume>40</volume><fpage>797</fpage><lpage>806</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0896-6273(03)00673-1</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Gomeza J, Ohno K, H&#x000fc;lsmann S, Armsen W, Eulenburg V, Richter DW, Laube B, Betz H (2003) Deletion of the mouse glycine transporter 2 results in a hyperekplexia phenotype and postnatal lethality. Neuron 40:797&#x0